## Proof

**Setup.** Parametrize points on the branch of $xy=1$ in the first quadrant as $(t, 1/t)$ for $t>0$. Let
$$A=(a,\,1/a),\quad P=(p,\,1/p),\quad B=(b,\,1/b),\qquad a<p<b.$$

**Step 1: Area of $\triangle APB$ and its maximizer.**

The signed area is
$$\frac12\Big|a\!\left(\tfrac1p-\tfrac1b\right)+p\!\left(\tfrac1b-\tfrac1a\right)+b\!\left(\tfrac1a-\tfrac1p\right)\Big|.$$
The expression inside simplifies to
$$(a-b)\cdot\frac{(p-a)(p-b)}{abp},$$
so, since $a<p<b$,
$$[\triangle APB]=\frac12\cdot\frac{(b-a)(p-a)(b-p)}{abp}=\frac{b-a}{2ab}\cdot\frac{(p-a)(b-p)}{p}.$$

Only the factor $g(p)=\dfrac{(p-a)(b-p)}{p}=(a+b)-p-\dfrac{ab}{p}$ depends on $p$. Setting $g'(p)=-1+\dfrac{ab}{p^2}=0$ gives
$$p=\sqrt{ab}.$$
Since $g''(p)=2ab/p^{3}>0$, this is the unique minimizer of $g$, hence the unique maximizer of the area. Thus $P=\bigl(\sqrt{ab},\,1/\sqrt{ab}\bigr)$.

**Step 2: Area between the hyperbola and chord $AP$.**

The chord through $(a,1/a)$ and $(p,1/p)$ has slope $-1/(ap)$, giving the line
$$\ell_{AP}(x)=\frac1a+\frac1p-\frac{x}{ap}.$$
The region bounded by the hyperbola $y=1/x$ and this chord over $[a,p]$ has area
$$S_1=\int_a^p\!\left(\frac1a+\frac1p-\frac{x}{ap}-\frac1x\right)dx
=\left[\frac{x}{a}+\frac{x}{p}-\frac{x^2}{2ap}-\ln x\right]_a^p.$$
Evaluating:
$$S_1=\left(\frac{p}{2a}+1-\ln p\right)-\left(1+\frac{a}{2p}-\ln a\right)=\frac{p}{2a}-\frac{a}{2p}+\ln\frac{a}{p}.$$

**Step 3: Area between the hyperbola and chord $PB$.**

By the identical computation with $[p,b]$ in place of $[a,p]$,
$$S_2=\int_p^b\!\left(\frac1p+\frac1b-\frac{x}{pb}-\frac1x\right)dx=\frac{b}{2p}-\frac{p}{2b}+\ln\frac{p}{b}.$$

**Step 4: Equality at the maximizing point $p=\sqrt{ab}$.**

Substituting $p=\sqrt{ab}$:

$$S_1=\frac{\sqrt{ab}}{2a}-\frac{a}{2\sqrt{ab}}+\ln\frac{a}{\sqrt{ab}}=\frac12\!\left(\sqrt{\frac ba}-\sqrt{\frac ab}\right)+\frac12\ln\frac{a}{b},$$

$$S_2=\frac{b}{2\sqrt{ab}}-\frac{\sqrt{ab}}{2b}+\ln\frac{\sqrt{ab}}{b}=\frac12\!\left(\sqrt{\frac ba}-\sqrt{\frac ab}\right)+\frac12\ln\frac{a}{b}.$$

Therefore $S_1=S_2$; that is, the region bounded by the hyperbola and chord $AP$ has the same area as the region bounded by the hyperbola and chord $PB$. $\blacksquare$

